An Nmap scan identified ports 22, 80, and 3000 as open.
Port 80 hosted a JavaScript compiler interface that displayed code execution results directly in the browser.
Enumeration of the application revealed information about the library used by the compiler tool.
Research identified a known vulnerability affecting this library, allowing sandbox escape and arbitrary code execution on the host system.
Exploitation of the vulnerability resulted in obtaining a reverse shell on the target machine.
Privilege escalation opportunities were limited with the compromised user account; however, enumeration showed that the user joshua had an associated Bash shell.
Further directory enumeration led to the discovery of a database file.
Using sqlite3, the database tables were inspected.
A hashed password belonging to joshua was identified. After determining the hash type, the password was successfully cracked using Hashcat.
Since user switching was restricted from the current shell, the recovered credentials were used to authenticate through the SSH service.
